If a clinician conducts an acute-pain-treatment with an opioid for an opioid-naive-patient during a few-day-period and the opioid-naive-patient is an outpatient then it is possible that an opioid-dosage-increase usually is unnecessary for the opioid-naive-patient.
If a clinician increases an opioid-dosage during an acute-pain-treatment for an opioid-naive-patient during a few-day-period then the clinician should closely monitor the opioid-naive-patient.
If a clinician conducts an acute-pain-treatment with an opioid for an opioid-naive-patient during a few-day-period and the clinician does not provably closely monitor the opioid-naive-patient then it is recommended that the clinician does not increase an opioid-dosage for the opioid-naive-patient.
Every opioid-dosage-increase can cause a respiratory-depression-risk.
